## Recommended quick read (order)
1. [Tech Doc](./tech-doc.mdc) for general engineering rules and workflow.
2. [Business Doc](./business-doc.mdc) for product behavior, UX, and business goals.
3. [Specific Tech Doc](./specific-tech-doc.mdc) for project-specific constraints.
4. [Specific Data Model](./specific-data-model.md) for DB model and data rules.

## Anti-duplication rule
- Cross-project generic rules belong in [tech-doc.mdc](./tech-doc.mdc) and [business-doc.mdc](./business-doc.mdc).
- Project-only details belong in `specific-*` files.
- If a rule is valid for other projects too, move it out of `specific-*` files.

## Documentation updates
- Every significant code change must update impacted docs in the same change set.
- Update README only when setup, build, deploy, or operating workflow changes.

### Which doc to update per change type

| Change type | Documents to update |
|---|---|
| New tables / columns / migrations | `docs/specific-data-model.md` |
| New routes / middleware / services / auth | `docs/specific-tech-backend-doc.mdc` |
| New Blade views / layouts / JS / CSS | `docs/specific-tech-frontend-doc.mdc` |
| Images / media handling | `docs/specific-tech-images-doc.md` |
| Business logic / UX / product behavior | `docs/business-doc.mdc` |
| Deploy / build / environment setup | `docs/DEPLOY.md` |
| Cross-project engineering rules | `docs/tech-doc.mdc` |

When making DB schema changes, **always** update both `specific-data-model.md` (tables/relations) and `specific-tech-backend-doc.mdc` (code patterns) if authorization or service logic is involved.
